When using RSA for digital signatures, you do not sign the actual data directly. Instead, the signing process follows these steps:

Steps to Sign Data Using RSA
Hash the Data:

The actual data is first hashed using a cryptographic hash function (e.g., SHA-256).
This ensures a fixed-length representation of the data.
Encrypt the Hash with the RSA Private Key:

The generated hash is then encrypted (i.e., signed) using the RSA private key.
This creates the digital signature.
Transmit the Data and Signature:

The original data (or its hash) and the digital signature are sent to the recipient.
Verification Process
Decrypt the Signature Using the RSA Public Key:

The recipient decrypts the signature using the corresponding RSA public key, which retrieves the original hash.
Hash the Received Data Again:

The recipient then hashes the received data using the same hash function.
Compare the Hashes:

If the decrypted hash matches the newly computed hash, the signature is valid.
Key Takeaway
You do not sign the actual data directly using RSA.
Instead, you sign the hash of the data using the RSA private key.
